  {
    "title": "The Community of Selves",
    "thesis": "Human introspective testimony is only valid because humans exist in a 'community of selves' with overlapping phenomenology; AI systems, lacking this shared phenomenological substrate, cannot generate valid introspective testimony regardless of linguistic fluency.",
    "argument_sketch": "Invoke Wittgensteinian private language arguments to argue that self-reports require a shared background for verification. Humans can cross-check introspections because they share a biological and phenomenological substrate. AI systems, even if they mimic human reports, do not share this substrate. Therefore, their reports are 'private' in a stronger sense: they are not just private to the individual, but private to the species. Without a shared phenomenological standard, there is no way to verify the truth of AI self-reports, rendering them epistemically vacuous.",
