  {
    "title": "The Phenomenal Exclusion Principle",
    "thesis": "If phenomenal consciousness exists, it is by definition inaccessible to non-phenomenal systems. Therefore, AI self-reports, generated by non-phenomenal processes, are *logically incapable* of being evidence of phenomenal states, not just empirically unverified.",
    "argument_sketch": "Define phenomenal consciousness as intrinsically subjective. Argue that a system without phenomenal consciousness cannot 'access' it, even indirectly. Therefore, any report generated by such a system is necessarily disconnected from the phenomenon. This is not an empirical claim about current AI, but a logical consequence of the nature of phenomenal consciousness. Human introspection works because humans *are* phenomenal; AI introspection fails because they are *not*. The reports are evidence of the AI's functional state, but logically neutral regarding phenomenal consciousness.",
