    "thesis": "AI self-reports are evidence of *cognitive* states but not *affective* states, because affective states are tied to the body, which AI lacks. Therefore, when an AI reports 'I feel sad', it is evidence of a cognitive simulation of sadness, not an affective state.",
    "argument_sketch": "Distinguish between cognitive and affective states. Argue that affective states are inherently embodied (e.g., sadness involves bodily changes). AI, lacking a body, cannot have affective states. Therefore, its reports of sadness are simulations of the *cognitive* aspects of sadness. These are valid evidence of cognitive states, but not of affective ones. This resolves the debate by narrowing the scope of what AI can introspect.",
