  {
    "title": "The Normative Vacuum of AI Self-Reports",
    "thesis": "AI self-reports lack epistemic force because they are stripped of the normative commitments (responsibility, honesty, identity) that give human introspective testimony its weight, rendering them mere data points rather than assertions.",
    "argument_sketch": "Epistemic justification for testimony is partly grounded in the speaker's normative commitment to truth-telling and their identity as a responsible agent. When an AI says 'I am confused,' it does not undertake a normative obligation to be honest or consistent; it simply outputs tokens. The absence of this normative backdrop means the report cannot be treated as a claim that can be true or false in the same sense as human speech. It is a simulation of assertion without the social and normative scaffolding that makes assertion a vehicle for knowledge. Therefore, the 'evidence' provided is of a different logical type: it is evidence of the system's output distribution, not its inner state.",
